    "description": "Iranian research identifies public parks as settings for physical activity whose use varies with age, facilities, environmental quality, and time of day. Evidence from Darab and Tehran helps explain these relationships, while providing no national measure of park-exercise participation.",
    "tags": ["Public parks", "Community sport", "Urban design", "Physical activity", "Accessibility"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T07:35:49Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T07:35:49Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Park Exercise in Iran: Activity, Access and Public Space",
      "description": "Explore evidence on exercise in Iranian parks, including research from Darab and Tehran and the importance of accessible public space."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "parks-as-activity-settings",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A setting for everyday movement",
        "content": "Park exercise belongs to the everyday side of Iranian sport: movement in public space rather than competition organized around teams, rankings, or medals. Its significance is partly that physical activity can take different forms. The World Health Organization includes walking, wheeling, active recreation, and play within its definition; participation need not involve a formal sporting skill. A park can therefore support activity without functioning as a conventional sports ground. [s1]\n\nThis also means that counting people in a park is different from measuring exercise. Sitting, socializing, walking, and vigorous movement are distinct uses of the same space. Iranian observational research makes that distinction explicit, examining what people do rather than treating every visit as an exercise session. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "darab-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What observations in Darab show",
        "content": "A study of public open spaces in Darab recorded users and their activity intensity. Children and teenagers were more frequently observed undertaking vigorous activity than other age groups, while seniors were more sedentary. Vigorous activity was especially evident in evening observations. These findings describe the observed settings and periods; they do not establish the daily routines of everyone living in the city. [s2]\n\nThe study associated larger spaces, better facilities, and more attractive surroundings with greater odds of higher activity intensity. Because the evidence is observational, it cannot establish that changing any single design feature would produce a particular increase in exercise. Nevertheless, it supports a practical distinction: the quality of a park matters alongside its existence. A map showing green space alone cannot reveal how actively that space is used. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "older-users",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Access includes confidence and comfort",
        "content": "Research in Tehran’s District 10 examined older residents’ perceptions of public outdoor spaces. It considered security, fear of falling, wayfinding, aesthetics, social surroundings, and attachment to place. The findings associated perceived environmental qualities with dimensions of health, suggesting that an exercise setting is also a social and psychological setting. [s3]\n\nFor interpreting park exercise, this shifts attention beyond equipment or paths to whether people feel able and willing to use the space. However, the study focused on older people already using public outdoor spaces in one district. Its findings cannot represent all Tehran residents, rural communities, or people whose barriers prevent them from visiting parks. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "interpreting-park-provision",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What park provision can establish",
        "content": "WHO identifies accessible public open spaces and community sport as components of policies that support physical activity. The Iranian studies provide local evidence for taking environmental quality seriously, but neither supplies a national inventory of functioning exercise equipment or a measure of regular park-exercise attendance. The useful conclusion is consequently specific: parks can provide opportunities for movement, while their contribution depends on who can use them and what actually happens there. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
