    "description": "Physical activity in later life includes everyday movement, recreation, and activities supporting strength and balance. Iranian survey research identifies substantial inactivity among older adults, while local urban research highlights the importance of usable and welcoming public space.",
    "tags": ["Older adults", "Healthy ageing", "Physical activity", "Accessibility", "Public health"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T07:35:49Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T07:35:49Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Physical Activity for Older People in Iran",
      "description": "Understand Iranian evidence on activity in later life, the limits of national survey findings, and the role of accessible community environments."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "activity-and-independence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Movement beyond organized sport",
        "content": "For older people, physical activity encompasses more than joining a sports club. Everyday movement and recreation sit alongside activities intended to maintain strength, balance, and functional ability. WHO’s guidelines treat older adults as a distinct population and emphasize that activity should reflect a person’s capabilities. The goal is meaningful participation across different levels of fitness, rather than a single sporting standard. [s1]\n\nThis distinction matters when reading Iranian evidence. A population survey measures reported activity under a defined questionnaire and threshold; it does not determine whether an individual should follow a particular exercise programme. Public-health guidance and national prevalence estimates answer different questions. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "national-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What the national survey establishes",
        "content": "A study published in 2026 analysed older participants in Iran’s 2021 STEPS survey, part of a standardized approach to monitoring risk factors for noncommunicable diseases. It used the WHO Global Physical Activity Questionnaire and found inactivity particularly prevalent among women and the oldest participants. Urban residence, obesity, depressive symptoms, and multiple chronic conditions were also associated with inactivity. [s2]\n\nThese associations should not be read as proof of cause. Health difficulties may restrict movement, while limited movement can coexist with other disadvantages. The survey also describes its reporting year, rather than conditions at the article’s publication date. Its value is identifying groups and circumstances requiring attention, not providing an annual measure of progress since the survey. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "environments-and-participation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The environment around the activity",
        "content": "Older adults’ opportunities also depend on their surroundings. Tehran research on public outdoor spaces examined perceived security, fear of falling, wayfinding, social environment, and attachment to place. These qualities were associated with dimensions of health in the study’s model. However, participants were already users of outdoor spaces in one district; people unable to reach those spaces were not adequately represented by that sampling frame. [s3]\n\nThe findings support asking whether a setting feels usable and welcoming, alongside asking whether a facility exists. They do not establish that the same relationships apply equally across Iranian cities and villages. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "public-health-framework",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A framework for community provision",
        "content": "WHO guidance combines aerobic movement with muscle strengthening and activities emphasizing functional balance. It also recognizes that some activity is preferable to none and that effort must be adjusted to functional ability. These are population-level principles, not an individualized prescription. [s1]\n\nRead together, the evidence suggests that community provision needs to accommodate different capabilities and circumstances. National research identifies unequal inactivity, local research examines environmental barriers, and international guidance explains the range of activity relevant to later life. No one source demonstrates that a particular Iranian programme has successfully resolved all these needs. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
