    "description": "Women’s sport in Iran encompasses international competition, domestic sporting pathways, recreational activity, and the institutions supporting participation. Verified achievements in taekwondo and futsal coexist with documented restrictions, making competitive results an incomplete measure of access.",
    "tags": ["Women’s sport", "Taekwondo", "Futsal", "Community sport", "Gender and sport", "Sports access"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T07:35:49Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T07:35:49Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Women’s Sport in Iran: Achievement, Participation and Access",
      "description": "An evidence-based introduction to Iranian women’s competitive achievements and the separate questions of participation, infrastructure, and access."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "different-measures",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Achievement and access answer different questions",
        "content": "Women’s sport in Iran requires attention to both sporting results and the conditions surrounding participation. Competition records establish what athletes achieved in specified events. They do not establish how easily women can enter a sport, obtain training, or attend matches as spectators. This distinction is visible when World Taekwondo’s record of an Iranian Olympic medal is read alongside independent reporting on restrictions affecting women’s access to football stadiums. [s1][s4]\n\nThe subject also extends beyond elite competitors. Research on physical activity among older Iranians identifies women as a group with particularly prevalent inactivity. That finding concerns everyday movement under a survey definition, rather than membership of sporting teams. It broadens the question from who wins medals to who has opportunities to be active. [s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s4", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "elite-results",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A documented Olympic achievement",
        "content": "Taekwondo, a combat sport contested in weight categories, provides a clear example of elite achievement. World Taekwondo’s athlete record lists Nahid Kiyanichandeh, also commonly known as Nahid Kiyani, as the silver medallist in the women’s under-57-kilogram event at the Paris 2024 Olympic Games. The result belongs to that athlete, event, and edition of the Games; it is not a ranking of women’s sport across Iran. [s1]\n\nFor international readers, careful naming matters because transliterations can differ between competition databases and reporting. Preserving the event and national representation alongside the athlete’s name helps identify the achievement without conflating people or competitions. The governing body’s record is used here for the sporting result, rather than as evidence about broader social conditions. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "futsal-development",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Futsal and sustained regional competition",
        "content": "Futsal is the small-sided form of football played on a hard court. Iran won the inaugural Asian women’s futsal championship in 2015, an achievement documented in the Asian Football Confederation’s retrospective. This offers another route into the subject: success in a team sport with its own competition history, rather than an extension of the men’s national football story. [s2]\n\nThe AFC’s report on the 2025 semifinal records Japan’s victory over Iran and notes that Iran had won the previous two editions. The result marks a change in that tournament’s competitive order. It should not be interpreted as evidence that Iran’s domestic participation, coaching quality, or resources had necessarily declined; those explanations would require separate investigation. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "spectators-and-participants",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Participation and spectatorship are separate forms of access",
        "content": "Being able to compete and being able to watch sport are different rights and experiences. Freedom House’s 2025 country report describes a longstanding exclusion of women from football stadiums alongside limited admissions to international matches in more recent years. Its account supports describing access as restricted and uneven during the period it covers, rather than claiming that every venue either admitted or excluded women under identical conditions. [s4]\n\nIt also cannot establish the admission arrangements for a particular fixture at the editorial cutoff. Event-specific access requires dated evidence. Similarly, a verified sporting result cannot settle questions about clothing requirements, travel permissions, broadcasting, or coaching arrangements. Those subjects need their own sources and careful distinctions between formal rules and documented practice. [s1][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "everyday-activity",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Beyond the competition calendar",
        "content": "The national study of older adults used Iran’s 2021 STEPS survey and reported greater inactivity among women than men. Its findings draw attention to participation outside organized sport, particularly in later life. However, the survey does not measure women’s league membership, identify the causes of every participation barrier, or describe conditions in 2026. [s5]\n\nA fuller account of women’s sport must therefore connect several kinds of evidence without treating them as interchangeable: results for elite performance, competition documentation for leagues, facility evidence for infrastructure, and population research for everyday activity. Coaching and management require additional attention because the people supporting sport shape opportunities as well as the athletes competing within it. These are the distinct questions organized by this section’s linked pages; several still require further research before a comprehensive national account is ready.",
        "source_ids": ["s5"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
      {
        "id": "s1",
